1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:12,560
They're the power that delivers the goods and the speed that pushes physics.

2
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:17,440
They connect oceans and move men deep below the Earth's surface.

3
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:21,000
They're crashed for safety and built to last.

4
00:00:21,860 --> 00:00:24,660
Powered by diesel, electricity, or steam.

5
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:27,660
They come in all shapes and sizes.

6
00:00:28,480 --> 00:00:29,680
Some even ride on air.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Now, it's Locomotives Plus on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:49,880 --> 00:00:52,920
Witness the pride of France.

9
00:00:52,921 --> 00:00:56,520
The train a grand vitesse, or TGV.

10
00:00:56,521 --> 00:00:59,520
Translated, it means high-speed train.

11
00:00:59,521 --> 00:01:03,520
But as with all trains, it's the locomotive that delivers the power.

12
00:01:03,521 --> 00:01:07,520
And as it rips across its 1,600-mile network,

13
00:01:07,521 --> 00:01:10,520
the TGV shows why it holds the title

14
00:01:10,521 --> 00:01:13,520
as the fastest locomotive on rails today.

15
00:01:13,521 --> 00:01:19,520
If you can figure out, we are running now at 300 kilometers per hour,

16
00:01:19,521 --> 00:01:23,520
and it seems like a dream. It's very easy.

17
00:01:23,521 --> 00:01:30,520
186 miles per hour is the average speed across much of the TGV network.

18
00:01:30,521 --> 00:01:36,520
The high tension wires racing by deliver the locomotive's power.

19
00:01:36,521 --> 00:01:41,520
From them, 25,000 volts of electricity is transformed into mechanical energy,

20
00:01:41,521 --> 00:01:45,520
which is then sent to motors on the train's wheels.

21
00:01:45,521 --> 00:01:51,520
Even at speeds like this, inside the TGV, the ride is smooth and steady.

22
00:01:51,521 --> 00:01:54,340
But if the engineer spots something on the track outside,

23
00:01:54,900 --> 00:01:56,740
there's little he can do to avoid hitting it.

24
00:01:57,220 --> 00:02:01,000
If you see something, then you will just knock it down.

25
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:06,280
Running at 300 kilometers, you would need 3 kilometers to stop.

26
00:02:06,281 --> 00:02:09,780
So if you can see something, obviously it's too late.

27
00:02:11,660 --> 00:02:15,280
In 1990, the TGV set the world speed record

28
00:02:15,281 --> 00:02:18,440
when it raced to an astounding 320 miles per hour.

29
00:02:18,441 --> 00:02:22,780
Then in 2007, train officials wanted more.

30
00:02:23,940 --> 00:02:29,480
So after some modifications, a TGV locomotive was teed up for another record attempt.

31
00:02:36,640 --> 00:02:44,180
To beat the existing 320 mile per hour record, the TGV would have to exceed 516 kilometers per hour.

32
00:03:17,240 --> 00:03:18,440
Takeunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnun nunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnun nunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnun nunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunnunn

33
00:03:18,441 --> 00:03:32,440
After the validation of the control, the official value of the speed of our march is 574.8 kmh.

34
00:03:32,441 --> 00:03:36,440
I repeat, 574.8 kmh.

35
00:03:36,441 --> 00:03:44,440
574 kmh equals 357 miles per hour and a new world record.

36
00:03:44,441 --> 00:03:49,940
The remarkable run proved once again that when it comes to speed, the TGV is king of the rails.

37
00:03:50,900 --> 00:03:55,240
The TGV still holds the record as the fastest locomotive on rails today.

38
00:03:56,460 --> 00:03:59,780
But this is what high-speed locomotives used to look like.

39
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:05,180
During the early 20th century, locomotives rarely hit 100 miles per hour.

40
00:04:06,860 --> 00:04:13,200
But when it comes to looks, most locomotive fans agree that none have been more smokin' than those powered by steam.

41
00:04:13,201 --> 00:04:18,400
Like this beauty built in 1924, the Baldwin Company's number 90.

42
00:04:20,240 --> 00:04:23,420
Still running on the Strasburg Railway in Strasburg, Pennsylvania.

43
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:27,700
Today, it's a tourist trend.

44
00:04:28,840 --> 00:04:33,420
It's still possible to climb on board for a ride back when locomotives like this

45
00:04:33,421 --> 00:04:36,800
helped pull America full steam ahead into the 20th century.

46
00:04:38,720 --> 00:04:40,400
Now we're ready to depart.

47
00:04:40,401 --> 00:04:47,400
Now I'm going to release the brakes and apply steam to the cylinders through the throttle.

48
00:04:47,401 --> 00:05:01,401
Now it's my job not to spin the wheels, so I want to just gradually apply power.

49
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:06,400
Applying power requires teamwork between the engineer and the man to his left,

50
00:05:06,401 --> 00:05:09,400
shoveling coal from the tender into the boiler.

51
00:05:09,401 --> 00:05:11,400
The fireman.

52
00:05:11,401 --> 00:05:16,400
Now the fireman has to get his fire nice and hot.

53
00:05:16,401 --> 00:05:19,400
By experience, he knows when to shovel.

54
00:05:19,401 --> 00:05:21,400
It's a coordinated effort.

55
00:05:21,401 --> 00:05:26,400
When I need this steam, he has to already have planned on producing.

56
00:05:26,401 --> 00:05:33,400
In the past, you'd burn about a ton and a half of coal per hour,

57
00:05:33,401 --> 00:05:36,900
and a fireman would be on duty for 12 hours.

58
00:05:37,740 --> 00:05:41,380
It could burn upwards of 12 to 15 tons of coal a day

59
00:05:41,381 --> 00:05:43,580
to be shoveling by hand from the tender.

60
00:05:44,340 --> 00:05:47,720
Igniting coal in the boiler is the first step in producing power.

61
00:05:50,260 --> 00:05:54,500
Since our camera would be fried by the 2,500 degree temperature in there,

62
00:05:54,960 --> 00:05:57,460
the best way to see how a steam engine works

63
00:05:57,461 --> 00:05:59,980
is by squeezing into a typical locomotive boiler.

64
00:05:59,981 --> 00:06:03,980
This one is under repair inside the Strasburg Railroad mechanical shop.

65
00:06:03,981 --> 00:06:08,980
Fireman throws the coal through the hole in the rear, lands in this box.

66
00:06:08,981 --> 00:06:13,980
This box is called the firebox. Surrounding the firebox is water.

67
00:06:13,981 --> 00:06:18,980
The water is all the way around here and is normally carried up to here.

68
00:06:18,981 --> 00:06:20,980
It always has to be above the top of the firebox.

69
00:06:20,981 --> 00:06:24,980
If it's not above the top of the firebox, the top of the firebox will melt.

70
00:06:24,981 --> 00:06:25,681
melt.

71
00:06:25,682 --> 00:06:33,180
So the heat from the fire can heat the firebox steel, boil the water, create the steam

72
00:06:33,181 --> 00:06:38,475
that goes up into the steam space, goes up into the steam dome.

73
00:06:38,476 --> 00:06:41,500
Within the steam dome is a throttle.

74
00:06:42,140 --> 00:06:46,560
When the engineer engages the throttle lever, steam is released into a pipe where it travels

75
00:06:46,561 --> 00:06:48,277
to the locomotive cylinders.

76
00:06:48,278 --> 00:06:52,080
Inside the cylinders, the steam pushes pistons back and forth.

77
00:06:52,081 --> 00:06:57,960
The pistons drive piston rods that in turn drive the locomotive's wheels.

78
00:06:59,780 --> 00:07:02,560
Regulating the steam pressure inside the boiler is critical.

79
00:07:03,400 --> 00:07:05,160
Too little and the train loses momentum.

80
00:07:06,100 --> 00:07:08,100
Too much and the boiler could explode.

81
00:07:09,360 --> 00:07:11,640
The steam boiler's design was inventive.

82
00:07:12,260 --> 00:07:16,640
But because coal is fed from the rear, its form creates a problem for the engineer.

83
00:07:17,200 --> 00:07:20,480
I can't see very well past this huge boiler.

84
00:07:20,481 --> 00:07:25,160
I have to lean out so I can see the biggest perspective ahead of the engine.

85
00:07:25,560 --> 00:07:29,260
I am constantly looking for anything that might obstruct us,

86
00:07:29,760 --> 00:07:34,560
including trees, including cattle, or anything else that might be problematic.

87
00:07:35,860 --> 00:07:38,960
That's why locomotive designers invented the cow catcher.

88
00:07:40,560 --> 00:07:43,000
Okay, it doesn't exactly catch cows.

89
00:07:43,440 --> 00:07:45,080
It kind of muscles them aside.

90
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:50,220
But the cow catcher can't shove aside the effects of the inevitable wear and tear

91
00:07:50,221 --> 00:07:55,080
that comes with a 100-ton locomotive thrashing around on 2-inch-wide rails.

92
00:07:57,440 --> 00:07:59,120
Tires especially take a beating.

93
00:07:59,960 --> 00:08:02,560
Like a car, a locomotive tire is attached to a wheel,

94
00:08:03,120 --> 00:08:05,460
except these tires are made of steel,

95
00:08:06,160 --> 00:08:08,140
and changing one takes some heat.

96
00:08:09,580 --> 00:08:13,620
The tire of a steam locomotive is shrunk onto the wheel.

97
00:08:14,460 --> 00:08:17,780
So in order to remove it, we put the fire ring on,

98
00:08:17,781 --> 00:08:21,060
We heat the tire up probably to 400 or 500 degrees.

99
00:08:21,960 --> 00:08:24,600
As it gets hotter, it will expand.

100
00:08:24,900 --> 00:08:27,860
Eventually, there'll be a gap between the tire and the wheel,

101
00:08:28,300 --> 00:08:33,520
and that tire will be able to swing and slip right off the wheel center.

102
00:08:35,840 --> 00:08:39,560
Add a fresh steel tire to the wheel, and it'll be ready to roll.

103
00:08:40,260 --> 00:08:42,360
Easy enough inside the mechanical shop.

104
00:08:43,220 --> 00:08:46,620
But in the old days, when things broke down out on the plains,

105
00:08:46,621 --> 00:08:49,620
The engineer did double duty as the mechanic.

106
00:08:49,621 --> 00:08:53,620
The technology was constantly breaking down.

107
00:08:53,621 --> 00:08:56,620
The engineer had to be aware of any problem arising

108
00:08:56,621 --> 00:08:59,620
by both sound and vision.

109
00:08:59,621 --> 00:09:02,620
And when that vision spotted something on the track,

110
00:09:02,621 --> 00:09:04,620
it's time to sound off.

111
00:09:06,620 --> 00:09:09,620
Every time the toot of a steam locomotive's whistle fills the air,

112
00:09:09,621 --> 00:09:13,620
it speaks in a unique, yet universal language.

113
00:09:13,621 --> 00:09:18,620
Now every engineer does his whistling a little bit differently.

114
00:09:18,621 --> 00:09:26,620
You'll now hear a crossing signal, two longs, a short and a long as we approach this crossing.

115
00:09:41,306 --> 00:09:47,940
By the 1930s, the sounds of the steam locomotive began to fade as designers looked to new technologies.

116
00:09:47,941 --> 00:09:58,920
In 1934, ElectroMotive launched a line of diesel-electric streamliner locomotives that were easier to maintain and cheaper to fuel than the coal-fed steamers.

117
00:10:01,320 --> 00:10:05,180
Today, diesel-electric locomotives are still the industry standard.

118
00:10:06,180 --> 00:10:10,200
But what exactly does it take to build one of these 200-ton powerhouses?

119
00:10:10,201 --> 00:10:21,540
For almost 100 years, the diesel locomotive has been the backbone of passenger and commercial

120
00:10:21,541 --> 00:10:25,220
rail transportation.

121
00:10:25,221 --> 00:10:32,600
Today there are more than 26,000 in service around the world.

122
00:10:32,601 --> 00:10:37,440
Companies like Electromotive Diesel in London, Ontario build hundreds of them every year

123
00:10:37,441 --> 00:10:40,080
for the international market.

124
00:10:40,081 --> 00:10:44,620
And every one starts from the bottom up with a single steel bed plate.

125
00:10:44,621 --> 00:10:47,900
What we see here is a domestic bed plate.

126
00:10:47,901 --> 00:10:54,260
It's approximately one and three quarter inches thick, 70 feet long, and six feet wide.

127
00:10:54,261 --> 00:10:58,120
And it weighs approximately 30,000 pounds.

128
00:10:58,121 --> 00:11:02,760
The bed plate begins its journey upside down, so welders and grinders can add sills and

129
00:11:02,761 --> 00:11:06,880
cross members to the underside.

130
00:11:06,881 --> 00:11:12,280
The upside down underframe eventually lands in the assembly area.

131
00:11:12,281 --> 00:11:16,480
Here's some of the major components are lowered into place.

132
00:11:16,481 --> 00:11:20,360
So what we see here is the compressed air tanks that supply the compressed air for the

133
00:11:20,361 --> 00:11:22,520
locomotive as well as the rail cars.

134
00:11:22,521 --> 00:11:28,640
And above us, what we see is the loading of a 5,000 gallon fuel tank, which is our domestic

135
00:11:28,641 --> 00:11:29,640
fuel tank.

136
00:11:29,641 --> 00:11:35,200
Tighten a few bolts and the underside of the locomotive is just about locked down.

137
00:11:35,201 --> 00:11:40,200
Meanwhile, workers are assembling other parts throughout the plant,

138
00:11:40,201 --> 00:11:43,200
like this upside-down wheel assembly.

139
00:11:43,201 --> 00:11:48,200
A combination of wheels, gear, axle, and electric motor.

140
00:11:48,201 --> 00:11:52,200
This is where the electric part of the diesel-electric locomotive comes in.

141
00:11:52,201 --> 00:11:56,200
We've got a 1,000-pound wheel at a 43-inch diameter.

142
00:11:56,201 --> 00:12:01,200
We've got an 83-tooth gear that weighs approximately 500 pounds

143
00:12:01,201 --> 00:12:06,700
with a 9 1⁄4-inch diameter axle sitting on an electric traction motor.

144
00:12:07,900 --> 00:12:11,320
Put three wheel assemblies together, and you've got a bogey.

145
00:12:11,760 --> 00:12:16,820
And this bogey is about to take its first spin, up and over, until it's right side up.

146
00:12:18,740 --> 00:12:23,200
Then it's off to final assembly, where the underframe is taking a twirl of its own.

147
00:12:23,760 --> 00:12:27,860
Set the underframe on top of the set of wheels, lower the cab into place,

148
00:12:28,200 --> 00:12:30,900
and all this Loco needs is some motive.

149
00:12:31,200 --> 00:12:33,700
Yep, this is the diesel part.

150
00:12:33,701 --> 00:12:39,100
What we see here is a diesel engine that we use on one of our locomotives.

151
00:12:39,101 --> 00:12:43,540
Compared to a normal car engine, a car engine runs around four to six cylinders, roughly

152
00:12:43,541 --> 00:12:48,480
about this size, compared to the cylinders that we use here, and we use 16 of them.

153
00:12:48,481 --> 00:12:53,100
When we talk about this engine, it weighs approximately 40,000 pounds, compared to a

154
00:12:53,101 --> 00:12:56,500
local car engine, which is much less than that, obviously.

155
00:12:56,501 --> 00:13:00,800
Also a local car engine would take around 200 horsepower, whereas this guy right here,

156
00:13:00,801 --> 00:13:03,956
workhorse runs around 4,300 horsepower.

157
00:13:03,957 --> 00:13:07,760
From here, we use the overhead cranes to remove

158
00:13:07,761 --> 00:13:12,960
the engine off the stand and deck it onto one of our units.

159
00:13:12,961 --> 00:13:19,380
On board, mechanics hook up the engine to an 8-ton alternator, and the diesel-electric

160
00:13:19,381 --> 00:13:26,300
locomotive is ready to rumble. And when it does, here's how it'll work.

161
00:13:26,301 --> 00:13:29,680
The diesel engine sends mechanical power to the alternator.

162
00:13:29,681 --> 00:13:33,540
The alternator transforms that power into electricity.

163
00:13:33,541 --> 00:13:38,820
The electricity is then sent to the electric traction motors on each wheel assembly.

164
00:13:38,821 --> 00:13:43,300
The motors turn the wheels, which apply power to the tracks.

165
00:13:43,301 --> 00:13:48,380
In the locomotive business, power on the track is measured in tractive effort.

166
00:13:48,381 --> 00:13:52,600
The more traction that can be applied between wheel and rail, the more pulling power on

167
00:13:52,601 --> 00:13:55,100
the locomotive.

168
00:13:55,101 --> 00:13:57,360
detractive effort is managing friction.

169
00:13:58,180 --> 00:14:00,780
And the secret lies in the simplest of substances.

170
00:14:01,660 --> 00:14:04,300
What we want here is we want to have good friction

171
00:14:04,301 --> 00:14:08,520
on the surface of the wheel where it contacts the actual rail.

172
00:14:08,860 --> 00:14:12,160
So how we increase the frictional force between these two surfaces

173
00:14:12,161 --> 00:14:17,660
is we use this nozzle and we actually pour sand into this area.

174
00:14:18,420 --> 00:14:20,800
What that does is it increases the frictional force,

175
00:14:21,360 --> 00:14:23,820
thus allowing more tractive effort to be placed on the rail.

176
00:14:23,821 --> 00:14:25,820
While the sand increases friction

177
00:14:25,821 --> 00:14:29,820
to the bottom of the wheel, just inches away,

178
00:14:29,821 --> 00:14:32,820
the wheel flange gets a rub with a clever little device

179
00:14:32,821 --> 00:14:34,820
that decreases friction.

180
00:14:34,821 --> 00:14:36,820
Here's the frictional force that we don't want.

181
00:14:36,821 --> 00:14:39,820
That's between this surface of the wheel

182
00:14:39,821 --> 00:14:41,820
and the inner surface of the rail.

183
00:14:41,821 --> 00:14:43,820
So what we've done to counteract that

184
00:14:43,821 --> 00:14:45,820
is we have this flange lube dispenser

185
00:14:45,821 --> 00:14:48,820
that has a graphite-based lubricant

186
00:14:48,821 --> 00:14:50,820
that makes contact with this surface.

187
00:14:50,821 --> 00:14:53,820
What it does is it applies it as the wheel turns

188
00:14:53,821 --> 00:14:55,820
and thus reduces the frictional force

189
00:14:55,821 --> 00:14:59,820
between this surface of the wheel and the rail itself.

190
00:14:59,821 --> 00:15:03,820
After all the steel is secured, the bogies are bolted,

191
00:15:03,821 --> 00:15:06,820
and the engine and motors are mounted,

192
00:15:06,821 --> 00:15:11,820
they make up one of the largest freight locomotives in the world.

193
00:15:11,821 --> 00:15:14,820
Here we are next to a completed domestic locomotive.

194
00:15:14,821 --> 00:15:19,820
The locomotive is 75 feet long, 16 feet high,

195
00:15:19,821 --> 00:15:21,820
and 10 feet wide.

196
00:15:21,821 --> 00:15:26,820
The locomotive itself weighs 425,000 pounds.

197
00:15:26,821 --> 00:15:29,820
It's really the workhorse out there in the industry.

198
00:15:29,821 --> 00:15:34,820
Before each of its workhorses is turned loose,

199
00:15:34,821 --> 00:15:36,820
it gets ridden hard out on the test track.

200
00:15:36,821 --> 00:15:38,820
So there's full throttle eight.

201
00:15:38,821 --> 00:15:42,820
You see the track of effort build up.

202
00:15:42,821 --> 00:15:45,820
And I should get around 4,500 horsepower.

203
00:15:45,821 --> 00:15:47,820
This is good.

204
00:15:47,821 --> 00:15:48,820
This is a good engine.

205
00:15:48,821 --> 00:15:50,820
It'll pull like crazy.

206
00:15:50,821 --> 00:15:51,820
Crazy?

207
00:15:51,821 --> 00:15:53,820
How's this for crazy?

208
00:15:55,820 --> 00:15:58,820
Nothing like a header to find a locomotive's weak spot.

209
00:16:03,820 --> 00:16:09,820
This is TTCI, the technology testing center outside Pueblo,

210
00:16:09,821 --> 00:16:15,820
Colorado, the place where the Federal Railroad Administration

211
00:16:15,821 --> 00:16:20,740
conducts research and locomotive manufacturers send technicians to test

212
00:16:20,741 --> 00:16:25,000
their latest designs by watching train wrecks and studying the effects for

213
00:16:25,001 --> 00:16:30,700
safety in future development. One of the things that we do here at TTCI is to

214
00:16:30,701 --> 00:16:35,440
conduct crash tests. That's one of the abilities that we have at an isolated

215
00:16:35,441 --> 00:16:39,600
site like that and we've done that right on the tracks right behind me. In the

216
00:16:39,601 --> 00:16:45,400
real world, locomotive crashes like this are rare. By studying crash test video,

217
00:16:45,401 --> 00:16:48,740
manufacturers like Electromotive Diesel, or EMD,

218
00:16:48,741 --> 00:16:51,900
are able to develop ways to reduce the risks.

219
00:16:51,901 --> 00:16:53,900
Even during the mother of all crashes,

220
00:16:53,901 --> 00:16:56,780
a head-on between two locomotives.

221
00:16:56,781 --> 00:17:00,780
For head-on collisions, we've developed an anti-climber.

222
00:17:00,781 --> 00:17:03,880
This whole structure is the anti-climber.

223
00:17:03,881 --> 00:17:08,560
It is designed to resist a strong upward force here and here

224
00:17:08,561 --> 00:17:12,400
and keep the coupler from coming up over the main structure here

225
00:17:12,401 --> 00:17:15,400
and the other car going into the cab.

226
00:17:15,401 --> 00:17:18,900
When two locomotives without the anticlimber collide head on,

227
00:17:19,440 --> 00:17:22,140
a locomotive with more momentum can slide straight up

228
00:17:22,141 --> 00:17:23,380
and into the other's cab.

229
00:17:25,960 --> 00:17:28,500
But if one locomotive has an anticlimber,

230
00:17:29,440 --> 00:17:32,120
the coupler from the other locomotive catches underneath it,

231
00:17:33,380 --> 00:17:36,300
stopping upward motion and diverting the energy laterally.

232
00:17:39,980 --> 00:17:43,200
The EMD anticlimber first used in 1989

233
00:17:43,201 --> 00:17:45,260
is now the industry standard.

234
00:17:45,400 --> 00:17:53,040
The crashworthiness of the locomotives used in North America is the best it's ever been,

235
00:17:53,041 --> 00:17:57,900
largely as a result of the kinds of tests that we do here, the activities of people

236
00:17:57,901 --> 00:18:02,860
like EMD in applying that information into the design of their cab.

237
00:18:02,861 --> 00:18:10,780
Out on the TTCI test track, EMD engineers are evaluating their newest design element,

238
00:18:10,781 --> 00:18:14,360
what they call the isolated cab.

239
00:18:14,361 --> 00:18:17,720
Their goal is to have the quietest cab in the business.

240
00:18:17,721 --> 00:18:20,320
We are trying to control the working environment.

241
00:18:20,321 --> 00:18:25,840
The whole purpose is to keep the noise down inside the cab from the 4,500 horsepower that's

242
00:18:25,841 --> 00:18:28,120
just 20 feet behind us.

243
00:18:28,121 --> 00:18:33,380
EMD has tried to reduce the noise and vibration inside the cab by separating it from the engine

244
00:18:33,381 --> 00:18:35,160
compartment.

245
00:18:35,161 --> 00:18:39,220
To record the decibel level, technicians have hung microphones at ear level throughout

246
00:18:39,221 --> 00:18:41,820
the cab.

247
00:18:41,821 --> 00:18:47,340
target is 80 decibels or less. Then it's time to open up the throttle to see if

248
00:18:47,341 --> 00:18:53,880
the isolated mounts work. Even at full throttle the sound level barely exceeds

249
00:18:53,881 --> 00:18:59,820
75 decibels, about the same as a normal conversation between two people and a

250
00:18:59,821 --> 00:19:06,060
victory for the EMD designers. And while they're here the crew is also collecting

251
00:19:06,061 --> 00:19:10,460
ride quality data by testing the cab for vibration.

252
00:19:10,461 --> 00:19:15,740
The whole cab has a number of sensors all over the cab, one of which is right here where

253
00:19:15,741 --> 00:19:22,400
we measure the cab's seat-based vibration in all three directions, up and down, four

254
00:19:22,401 --> 00:19:25,440
and a half, side to side.

255
00:19:25,441 --> 00:19:28,820
Technicians record the data in a test car behind the locomotive.

256
00:19:28,821 --> 00:19:34,840
Later, EMD will use the data to develop even more ways to improve its locomotives and keep

257
00:19:34,841 --> 00:19:38,780
its operating engineers safe.

258
00:19:38,781 --> 00:19:40,940
In another area of the test facility,

259
00:19:40,941 --> 00:19:46,380
TTCI offers locomotive makers a way to look through steel

260
00:19:46,381 --> 00:19:50,160
with its automated cracked wheel detection system.

261
00:19:50,161 --> 00:19:52,500
The system uses sound waves to take a snapshot

262
00:19:52,501 --> 00:19:54,560
of a locomotive's wheels as they pass

263
00:19:54,561 --> 00:19:56,920
through an ultrasonic sensor.

264
00:19:56,921 --> 00:20:00,000
Right now, this first wheel is going to be picked up

265
00:20:00,001 --> 00:20:01,600
by this inspection site.

266
00:20:01,601 --> 00:20:04,340
It's going to follow it all the way through.

267
00:20:04,341 --> 00:20:07,080
Once it follows it through, it's going to reset itself,

268
00:20:07,081 --> 00:20:09,540
set up for the next wheel that comes through.

269
00:20:09,541 --> 00:20:10,460
It'll pick it up.

270
00:20:10,461 --> 00:20:14,020
It'll send ultrasonic energy up through the wheel

271
00:20:14,021 --> 00:20:16,020
and inspect the wheel for cracks.

272
00:20:16,021 --> 00:20:18,740
And then that information will be transmitted

273
00:20:18,741 --> 00:20:20,820
into our computer bungalow.

274
00:20:20,821 --> 00:20:22,660
Inside the bungalow, the results appear

275
00:20:22,661 --> 00:20:25,200
on a computer screen in real time.

276
00:20:25,201 --> 00:20:27,740
Each rectangular box represents a wheel.

277
00:20:27,741 --> 00:20:29,880
The lines reveal some type of flaw.

278
00:20:29,881 --> 00:20:32,280
Right here, you're showing some thermal cracks

279
00:20:32,281 --> 00:20:33,720
in the locomotive wheel.

280
00:20:33,721 --> 00:20:35,780
Here you're showing a horizontal crack.

281
00:20:35,781 --> 00:20:39,420
So then it triggers the maintenance crew

282
00:20:39,421 --> 00:20:42,520
on what they need to do in order to correct that.

283
00:20:42,521 --> 00:20:48,600
Whereas TTCI exists to help find a locomotive's flaws,

284
00:20:48,601 --> 00:20:51,580
a network of service stations across the United States

285
00:20:51,581 --> 00:20:53,700
stands ready to fix them.

286
00:20:53,701 --> 00:20:56,340
One of the best is the CSX maintenance facility

287
00:20:56,341 --> 00:20:58,980
in Waycross, Georgia.

288
00:20:58,981 --> 00:21:02,340
CSX transportation runs 20,000 miles of track

289
00:21:02,341 --> 00:21:04,580
in the United States.

290
00:21:04,581 --> 00:21:07,080
Every day, thousands of CSX locomotives

291
00:21:07,081 --> 00:21:09,740
traverse the country hauling freight.

292
00:21:09,741 --> 00:21:13,620
Keeping them operational is crucial to the bottom line.

293
00:21:13,621 --> 00:21:14,920
Time is money.

294
00:21:14,921 --> 00:21:18,760
And these locomotives cost about $2 million a piece.

295
00:21:18,761 --> 00:21:22,600
And if they're setting, we're not utilizing that asset.

296
00:21:22,601 --> 00:21:25,620
We can service as many as 12 at one time.

297
00:21:25,621 --> 00:21:28,680
This is simply a service station for locomotives.

298
00:21:28,681 --> 00:21:30,540
And this service station is built for speed.

299
00:21:32,340 --> 00:21:36,840
It's kind of like a NASCAR pit stop for locomotives.

300
00:21:36,841 --> 00:21:38,680
The moment a locomotive pulls in,

301
00:21:38,681 --> 00:21:41,340
a team of workers moves into place.

302
00:21:41,341 --> 00:21:45,420
The aim is to get it in and out in under an hour.

303
00:21:45,421 --> 00:21:48,180
This is where it starts here at the Locomotive Services Center.

304
00:21:48,181 --> 00:21:51,160
We'll service anywhere from 75 to 100 locomotives

305
00:21:51,161 --> 00:21:52,660
at this facility per day.

306
00:21:52,661 --> 00:21:54,200
Here's where the sand will be going in.

307
00:21:54,201 --> 00:21:57,140
It'll be coming in through a funnel that he'll put in.

308
00:21:57,141 --> 00:21:58,860
Here we have a gentleman that's going to be changing

309
00:21:58,861 --> 00:22:01,840
a flange lube stick to help with the flange wear.

310
00:22:01,841 --> 00:22:03,840
We're going in the curves on the track.

311
00:22:03,841 --> 00:22:06,200
Here the gentleman's checking the oil and the water.

312
00:22:06,201 --> 00:22:10,800
We're going to make sure everything is in good running repair with the diesel engine.

313
00:22:10,801 --> 00:22:13,260
Here we are adding fuel to the locomotive.

314
00:22:13,261 --> 00:22:19,860
The locomotive fuel tank will hold anywhere from about 1,500 gallons up to 4,500 gallons.

315
00:22:19,861 --> 00:22:27,820
At this facility, we will pump about 100,000 gallons of diesel fuel a day.

316
00:22:27,821 --> 00:22:31,120
as workers handle the basics from above.

317
00:22:31,121 --> 00:22:34,820
Others are busy in the service pit below.

318
00:22:34,821 --> 00:22:36,460
As they're working up above, they're also

319
00:22:36,461 --> 00:22:39,900
going to be changing brake shoes down below.

320
00:22:39,901 --> 00:22:41,860
Warren is going to be pulling this brake shoe out,

321
00:22:41,861 --> 00:22:44,900
going to be replacing it with the new brake shoe.

322
00:22:44,901 --> 00:22:48,280
He has to remove the brake key, take the old brake shoe out,

323
00:22:48,281 --> 00:22:50,080
replace it with the new brake shoe,

324
00:22:50,081 --> 00:22:52,420
and then drop the brake key back in that holds the brake shoe

325
00:22:52,421 --> 00:22:54,380
in place.

326
00:22:54,381 --> 00:22:56,180
And it's ready to go.

327
00:22:56,181 --> 00:23:00,220
Here we have a worn brake shoe that is in need of being replaced.

328
00:23:00,221 --> 00:23:02,860
Here we have a new brake shoe that we will put in its place.

329
00:23:02,861 --> 00:23:05,820
The new brake shoe should last approximately three to four weeks,

330
00:23:05,821 --> 00:23:09,120
depending on the amount of traffic the locomotive is involved in.

331
00:23:11,900 --> 00:23:15,140
Of course, there are times when some heavy lifting is required.

332
00:23:15,141 --> 00:23:18,660
For those cases, locomotives make a trip to the back shop,

333
00:23:18,661 --> 00:23:21,580
where mechanics carry out everything from cosmetic repair

334
00:23:21,581 --> 00:23:24,300
to complete engine rebuilds.

335
00:23:24,301 --> 00:23:25,980
We do lots of accident damage.

336
00:23:26,420 --> 00:23:30,900
That could be crossing accidents that happen out on the line of road.

337
00:23:31,360 --> 00:23:36,340
We change main alternators, and we just do lots and lots of work,

338
00:23:36,660 --> 00:23:39,320
more or less rebuild the locomotive in that area.

339
00:23:41,020 --> 00:23:43,760
Once most locomotives are cleared to return to action,

340
00:23:45,500 --> 00:23:49,400
they get a final sprucing up with a quick shower in the CSX wash.

341
00:23:50,660 --> 00:23:53,060
It gets a lot of the road film off of them,

342
00:23:53,061 --> 00:23:57,140
because after all, CSX, that's what's on the side of the locomotives.

343
00:23:57,220 --> 00:24:00,900
We're proud of our company, and we certainly want to look good in the public's eye.

344
00:24:06,980 --> 00:24:09,280
Unlike the little engine that thought it could,

345
00:24:09,960 --> 00:24:13,600
these mini locomotives along the Panama Canal know they can.

346
00:24:15,860 --> 00:24:18,080
Nearly 14,000 times a year,

347
00:24:18,220 --> 00:24:21,580
they slowly and steadily guide ships through the three locks

348
00:24:21,581 --> 00:24:24,220
that connect the Pacific and Atlantic Oceans.

349
00:24:28,520 --> 00:24:31,060
Here, size matters less than precision.

350
00:24:31,900 --> 00:24:37,120
The locomotives measure just 13 feet high, 10 feet wide, and 35 feet long.

351
00:24:37,700 --> 00:24:40,500
But there's nothing small about the economic stakes of their mission.

352
00:24:41,760 --> 00:24:44,760
The toll that is charged depends on the size of the ship.

353
00:24:46,300 --> 00:24:51,160
The average for cargo and container vessels is about $450,000.

354
00:24:51,580 --> 00:24:53,980
but some fees can go as high as $1 million.

355
00:24:54,940 --> 00:24:57,980
At these prices, the nearly 40 shipping companies

356
00:24:57,981 --> 00:25:00,180
that send vessels through the canal every day

357
00:25:00,181 --> 00:25:02,060
expect to do it safely and efficiently,

358
00:25:02,720 --> 00:25:07,180
like this 70,000-ton Panamanian cargo ship

359
00:25:07,181 --> 00:25:08,540
coming in from the Pacific.

360
00:25:09,100 --> 00:25:11,660
The chamber is very small for this ship,

361
00:25:12,020 --> 00:25:13,140
and it's a slow operation,

362
00:25:13,580 --> 00:25:15,460
and the locomotives are very critical

363
00:25:15,461 --> 00:25:17,460
for handling a ship of this size.

364
00:25:18,460 --> 00:25:20,580
As the ship approaches the lock's mouth,

365
00:25:20,581 --> 00:25:23,380
Eight locomotives, four on the starboard side,

366
00:25:23,480 --> 00:25:27,680
four to the port, move into position.

367
00:25:27,780 --> 00:25:30,520
The ship is 106 feet wide.

368
00:25:30,620 --> 00:25:34,220
The lock chamber is just 110.

369
00:25:34,320 --> 00:25:36,900
And for the next half mile, the little locos

370
00:25:37,000 --> 00:25:41,000
will be guiding this ship to the end of the lock.

371
00:25:41,100 --> 00:25:42,700
The carefully choreographed journey

372
00:25:42,800 --> 00:25:45,640
begins as canal ship personnel throw a line

373
00:25:45,740 --> 00:25:49,140
to grab two steel cables attached to hydraulic winches

374
00:25:49,141 --> 00:25:51,140
on the side of the locomotives.

375
00:25:51,141 --> 00:25:55,140
The idea is to get the cables of the locomotives onto the ship.

376
00:25:55,141 --> 00:25:58,140
As you can see, there are two locomotives

377
00:25:58,141 --> 00:26:00,140
taking care of the bow of the ship.

378
00:26:00,141 --> 00:26:06,140
Then we're going to see two more taking care of the back of the ship, the stern.

379
00:26:06,141 --> 00:26:10,140
All eight locomotives are under the direction of a specially trained pilot

380
00:26:10,141 --> 00:26:13,140
aboard the ship, who works for the canal.

381
00:26:13,141 --> 00:26:17,140
His first command to the locomotive operators is to pull their cables tight

382
00:26:17,141 --> 00:26:20,040
until the ship is securely tethered in the center of the lock.

383
00:26:21,520 --> 00:26:23,940
Then its ships and locomotives ahoy,

384
00:26:25,060 --> 00:26:27,300
as the pilot gradually powers his ship forward

385
00:26:27,301 --> 00:26:30,040
while directing the locomotives to move in unison with him.

386
00:26:32,880 --> 00:26:36,260
From here on, it's all about keeping the sides of the ship's steel hull

387
00:26:36,261 --> 00:26:38,600
off the cement walls that line the lock.

388
00:26:39,880 --> 00:26:42,480
As you can see, the ship is very close to the wall.

389
00:26:43,240 --> 00:26:45,140
The same thing is happening on the other side,

390
00:26:45,141 --> 00:26:47,700
So they should be able to coordinate with the pilot

391
00:26:47,701 --> 00:26:51,160
to keep the ship as much in the center as they can.

392
00:26:51,260 --> 00:26:52,800
There's only two feet on each side.

393
00:26:53,360 --> 00:26:57,820
Any little mistake, you can rub the ship against the wall and cause damage.

394
00:26:59,440 --> 00:27:02,460
Top speed for these locomotives is 10 miles per hour.

395
00:27:03,000 --> 00:27:05,640
But with a ship this size and so little room for error,

396
00:27:06,120 --> 00:27:08,840
they crawl along at about 3 miles per hour.

397
00:27:11,020 --> 00:27:13,040
Even at this speed, mistakes are costly.

398
00:27:13,041 --> 00:27:17,720
So to minimize any confusion between ship captain and locomotive operator,

399
00:27:17,721 --> 00:27:21,920
there are no conversations, just orders and action.

400
00:27:43,040 --> 00:27:44,235
control for each cable.

401
00:27:44,236 --> 00:27:48,080
He's holding the ship in the center together with his partner on the other

402
00:27:48,081 --> 00:27:53,216
side. Asking number one center to coil. They don't have to do anything here.

403
00:27:53,217 --> 00:27:55,040
Just keep the cables tight.

404
00:27:55,041 --> 00:27:57,811
He's moving ahead now a little bit.

405
00:27:57,812 --> 00:28:02,800
While the ship remains under its own power, the locomotives are

406
00:28:02,801 --> 00:28:07,164
propelled with electricity via a shoe that's connected to the rails.

407
00:28:07,165 --> 00:28:08,640
The rails are energized

408
00:28:08,641 --> 00:28:10,480
with 480 volts.

409
00:28:10,481 --> 00:28:14,800
In the middle of the track, a steel gear pulls the locomotive along.

410
00:28:14,801 --> 00:28:16,960
The electricity comes from below,

411
00:28:16,961 --> 00:28:19,360
inside a specially designed power station

412
00:28:19,361 --> 00:28:21,440
that runs the length of the track.

413
00:28:21,441 --> 00:28:23,440
We are inside of the machinery tunnel,

414
00:28:23,441 --> 00:28:25,600
right underneath of the locomotive tracks

415
00:28:25,601 --> 00:28:28,320
that runs all along the log walls.

416
00:28:28,321 --> 00:28:31,680
And here is where the locomotives will get their power from.

417
00:28:33,920 --> 00:28:36,400
This is a transformer that brings the voltage down

418
00:28:36,401 --> 00:28:39,160
from 2,400 volts down to 480.

419
00:28:39,161 --> 00:28:41,900
The power is required by the locomotive.

420
00:28:41,901 --> 00:28:44,240
These are the main breakers for the power

421
00:28:44,241 --> 00:28:46,920
that is fed to the locomotive above us.

422
00:28:46,921 --> 00:28:48,820
Up above, the Panamanian ship

423
00:28:48,821 --> 00:28:51,620
is now inside the lock chamber.

424
00:28:51,621 --> 00:28:54,260
As the lock fills with water and the ship rises,

425
00:28:54,261 --> 00:28:58,560
it's steady as she goes for the locomotive operators.

426
00:28:58,561 --> 00:29:00,500
During this time, the locomotives just have to keep

427
00:29:00,501 --> 00:29:03,300
the ship in the position with the cables tight.

428
00:29:03,301 --> 00:29:05,900
That way we will avoid hitting the walls

429
00:29:05,901 --> 00:29:08,440
and we keep the ship safe.

430
00:29:08,441 --> 00:29:12,140
Keeping a 140 million pound ship centered in the lock

431
00:29:12,141 --> 00:29:13,840
requires some tension.

432
00:29:13,841 --> 00:29:17,680
Most of it is focused on the cables.

433
00:29:17,681 --> 00:29:19,940
The cables are routinely tested to withstand

434
00:29:19,941 --> 00:29:25,460
twice the amount of stress they'll ever need out on the lock.

435
00:29:25,461 --> 00:29:27,180
For the locomotive engineers,

436
00:29:27,181 --> 00:29:30,220
stress comes from the one thing they all fear,

437
00:29:30,221 --> 00:29:33,120
a dip in the drink.

438
00:29:33,121 --> 00:29:34,320
The cables are very tight,

439
00:29:34,321 --> 00:29:36,720
so the locomotives are pulling very strong.

440
00:29:36,721 --> 00:29:39,520
To avoid the locomotive overturning into the water,

441
00:29:39,521 --> 00:29:40,960
we have the safety wheels.

442
00:29:40,961 --> 00:29:42,560
That's why we use the safety wheels.

443
00:29:42,561 --> 00:29:44,960
They are attached to this piece of track,

444
00:29:44,961 --> 00:29:47,760
so the locomotive is always secure to the track.

445
00:29:47,761 --> 00:29:50,000
No matter how much force they put on the cables,

446
00:29:50,001 --> 00:29:53,000
they will always stay on the track.

447
00:29:53,001 --> 00:29:54,880
Of the hundreds of thousands of passages

448
00:29:54,881 --> 00:29:57,000
during the canal's history,

449
00:29:57,001 --> 00:30:00,640
just one locomotive has been pulled sideways into the canal,

450
00:30:00,641 --> 00:30:03,120
and that was due to human error.

451
00:30:03,121 --> 00:30:05,660
A closer look reveals the virtually accident-proof

452
00:30:05,661 --> 00:30:07,620
safeguarding system.

453
00:30:07,621 --> 00:30:09,320
Here we are underneath the locomotive

454
00:30:09,321 --> 00:30:10,820
in the maintenance pit.

455
00:30:10,821 --> 00:30:12,560
Here we can find the safety wheels.

456
00:30:12,561 --> 00:30:14,460
These are the ones that prevent the locomotive

457
00:30:14,461 --> 00:30:17,160
from tilting and going into the water.

458
00:30:17,161 --> 00:30:20,780
The gates of the lock are opened.

459
00:30:20,781 --> 00:30:22,580
Like countless ships before it,

460
00:30:22,581 --> 00:30:25,680
the cargo carrier is about to leave its tight squeeze here

461
00:30:25,681 --> 00:30:29,180
for the open waters of the Pacific Ocean.

462
00:30:29,181 --> 00:30:31,720
The ship is ready to get out of the lock.

463
00:30:31,721 --> 00:30:34,060
we're reaching the end of the sidewalk.

464
00:30:34,061 --> 00:30:36,220
Three, somewhere here, I'm thinking number three.

465
00:30:36,221 --> 00:30:37,120
Survey.

466
00:30:37,121 --> 00:30:39,360
He just asked number three to release the wires

467
00:30:39,361 --> 00:30:40,660
and cast off.

468
00:30:40,661 --> 00:30:42,860
So he's going to cast off the number three.

469
00:30:42,861 --> 00:30:44,160
The guy's in front of us.

470
00:30:44,161 --> 00:30:45,060
Cast off, thank you.

471
00:30:45,061 --> 00:30:45,761
Forsythe.

472
00:30:45,762 --> 00:30:47,960
He just asked Forsythe us to cast off.

473
00:30:47,961 --> 00:30:48,880
On a force center.

474
00:30:48,881 --> 00:30:51,480
He's going to release the wires and cast off.

475
00:30:51,481 --> 00:30:54,680
He's giving the signal so the deckhands can release

476
00:30:54,681 --> 00:30:57,880
the cable from the bit on the ship.

477
00:30:57,881 --> 00:31:00,980
They're going to let go any time.

478
00:31:00,981 --> 00:31:01,681
And here they come.

479
00:31:01,682 --> 00:31:02,382
One center.

480
00:31:03,420 --> 00:31:05,140
OK, so we're off the ship.

481
00:31:06,720 --> 00:31:08,920
45 minutes after it entered the lock,

482
00:31:09,200 --> 00:31:11,460
another vessel has completed safe passage

483
00:31:11,461 --> 00:31:13,640
for the Little Canal locomotives.

484
00:31:14,600 --> 00:31:17,660
A long line of customers is waiting at the lock's entrance.

485
00:31:20,100 --> 00:31:22,200
But if you enter one of these locomotives,

486
00:31:22,660 --> 00:31:24,820
you'll need to take an elevator to get to it,

487
00:31:25,540 --> 00:31:27,900
more than 1,000 feet underground.

488
00:31:30,980 --> 00:31:33,280
More than 1,000 feet underground.

489
00:31:34,460 --> 00:31:37,040
Inside the Bailey Mine in southwestern Pennsylvania,

490
00:31:38,140 --> 00:31:40,760
a fleet of some of the most unusual locomotives

491
00:31:40,761 --> 00:31:42,840
is busy ferrying miners and equipment

492
00:31:42,841 --> 00:31:46,740
through a labyrinth of tunnels that spans hundreds of miles.

493
00:31:47,740 --> 00:31:51,320
For decades, the fleet was powered by three different sources,

494
00:31:51,880 --> 00:31:55,200
external electrical, battery, and diesel.

495
00:31:57,540 --> 00:31:59,760
The electric locomotives were attached

496
00:31:59,761 --> 00:32:02,860
to an overhead trolley line and its obvious limitations.

497
00:32:04,700 --> 00:32:07,780
This is a very old battery trolley unit.

498
00:32:08,900 --> 00:32:12,420
It's powered, it gets all its power from the overhead trolley line.

499
00:32:13,380 --> 00:32:16,660
That technology's been used in the mines for decades.

500
00:32:17,200 --> 00:32:21,840
The problem with the older technology is he has to be underneath this to operate.

501
00:32:22,360 --> 00:32:25,040
When he goes away from a trolley line, he's dead.

502
00:32:25,041 --> 00:32:30,880
Another drawback to the outdated trolley line is the 300 volts hanging from above,

503
00:32:31,300 --> 00:32:34,640
just waiting to take a bite out of anyone who accidentally touches it.

504
00:32:35,360 --> 00:32:37,860
Power outages create yet another problem.

505
00:32:38,720 --> 00:32:44,680
If you have any power problems or anything drops out, then that piece of equipment has no use.

506
00:32:45,300 --> 00:32:48,340
I mean, any power problem in the mine or if you had to get out, you would have none.

507
00:32:48,780 --> 00:32:53,600
The mine's battery-powered locomotives eliminated the need for an overhead trolley line.

508
00:32:55,040 --> 00:32:57,800
But locomotive batteries don't keep going and going.

509
00:32:58,800 --> 00:33:01,520
You have to actually take it into a charging station and charge it.

510
00:33:01,800 --> 00:33:07,100
You're losing the use of your piece of equipment while you're actually charging it.

511
00:33:08,100 --> 00:33:12,340
Everything considered, the power source that most miners prefer may surprise you.

512
00:33:13,220 --> 00:33:13,920
Diesel.

513
00:33:14,560 --> 00:33:15,680
Diesel is much safer.

514
00:33:16,340 --> 00:33:21,780
You no longer have the hazards of any short circuits in your coal mine and the potential for mine fires.

515
00:33:21,820 --> 00:33:23,840
With diesel, you're totally in control.

516
00:33:23,841 --> 00:33:28,540
I expect to have Bailey Mine converted entirely, 100% to diesel,

517
00:33:28,660 --> 00:33:32,080
because I do believe diesel is the future for track-mounted equipment in the coal mine.

518
00:33:33,880 --> 00:33:35,960
In fact, that's exactly what happened.

519
00:33:37,100 --> 00:33:40,600
Today, the Bailey Mine's fleet is run solely on diesel power.

520
00:33:42,420 --> 00:33:45,420
Wondering how Bailey's miners coexist deep underground

521
00:33:45,421 --> 00:33:48,780
with a herd of diesel-burning locomotives spewing out exhaust?

522
00:33:49,700 --> 00:33:52,380
Brookville Equipment Corporation in Brookville, Pennsylvania,

523
00:33:52,381 --> 00:33:55,880
has been building underground diesel locomotives since 1980.

524
00:33:56,960 --> 00:33:59,180
And they have the answer to the diesel question.

525
00:34:00,460 --> 00:34:04,320
A lot needs to go into the safety and the health of the people working in that environment.

526
00:34:04,820 --> 00:34:07,740
This is a 170-horsepower diesel engine.

527
00:34:08,200 --> 00:34:10,540
It's MSHA-approved for underground use.

528
00:34:11,180 --> 00:34:14,280
The engine manufacturer has to take it through a series of tests

529
00:34:14,281 --> 00:34:18,420
where they measure the particulates and they give it a certification.

530
00:34:19,380 --> 00:34:22,260
Brookville has also installed a state-of-the-art filtering system.

531
00:34:22,380 --> 00:34:24,640
sure to keep miners breathing easy.

532
00:34:25,640 --> 00:34:28,460
To run the diesel engines underground in a coal mine,

533
00:34:28,620 --> 00:34:31,100
you have to have an additional exhaust treatment system.

534
00:34:31,340 --> 00:34:33,160
The exhaust comes out and goes through a catalyst,

535
00:34:33,640 --> 00:34:36,120
which breaks apart the diesel particulate matter.

536
00:34:36,320 --> 00:34:39,480
The exhaust gas comes up into this filter housing,

537
00:34:39,640 --> 00:34:43,820
where a replaceable paper filter element is used to capture

538
00:34:43,821 --> 00:34:49,380
approximately 95% of the diesel soot that is emitted from the engine.

539
00:34:49,620 --> 00:34:52,240
And that's why we don't smell diesel engine in here.

540
00:34:52,380 --> 00:34:53,540
diesel fuel.

541
00:34:53,541 --> 00:34:56,120
You put it in the box that the new one comes in,

542
00:34:56,121 --> 00:35:00,620
and it's acceptable to any landfill.

543
00:35:00,621 --> 00:35:03,360
For a typical Brookbill diesel locomotive,

544
00:35:03,361 --> 00:35:09,440
nearly 2,000 parts are cut, drilled, forged, and assembled,

545
00:35:09,441 --> 00:35:13,800
while the diminutive frames get built separately.

546
00:35:13,801 --> 00:35:17,440
Engine sits in the front here, transmission in the center.

547
00:35:17,441 --> 00:35:19,140
You can see the two wheel wells.

548
00:35:19,141 --> 00:35:24,960
And these are grids for airflow and the operator compartment up front.

549
00:35:25,240 --> 00:35:28,140
These are 23-ton locomotives.

550
00:35:29,440 --> 00:35:34,600
Down in the Bailey Mine, a Brookville diesel-powered personnel carrier is filling up for the morning commute.

551
00:35:36,600 --> 00:35:39,880
You can see the men in here. They're getting ready for their shift to begin.

552
00:35:40,180 --> 00:35:42,340
You can see the operator station of this vehicle.

553
00:35:43,160 --> 00:35:47,980
He has all the controls that tell him full function of the engine, the brake and throttle.

554
00:35:47,981 --> 00:35:49,980
these guys got to go to work.

555
00:35:51,240 --> 00:35:54,480
They can go 10 to 12 miles an hour on good track.

556
00:35:55,120 --> 00:35:56,460
It's a whole lot faster than walking.

557
00:35:58,480 --> 00:36:01,660
Every day, 80 different underground vehicles

558
00:36:01,661 --> 00:36:02,900
travel throughout the mine.

559
00:36:03,840 --> 00:36:05,940
But what happens if one of them derails?

560
00:36:07,400 --> 00:36:08,900
Brookville's got that covered, too.

561
00:36:09,960 --> 00:36:12,540
Now operators can get things back on track

562
00:36:12,541 --> 00:36:14,360
with the rail walker.

563
00:36:14,361 --> 00:36:21,360
The old way is using a hand jack or a railroad jack.

564
00:36:21,361 --> 00:36:25,360
Very labor intensive and relatively dangerous.

565
00:36:25,361 --> 00:36:28,360
The new way is hydraulically.

566
00:36:28,361 --> 00:36:31,360
So what we did, we hydraulically lifted the locomotive

567
00:36:31,361 --> 00:36:33,360
by putting a foot down on the ground,

568
00:36:33,361 --> 00:36:36,360
raising the locomotive, and moving it sideways.

569
00:36:36,361 --> 00:36:38,360
Position it back over the rail.

570
00:36:38,361 --> 00:36:40,360
It's moving back into position.

571
00:36:40,361 --> 00:36:46,020
the wheels being lowered gently in a controlled manner back down on the rail.

572
00:36:46,800 --> 00:36:50,860
With innovations like the rail walker and the clean-burning diesel engine,

573
00:36:51,940 --> 00:36:55,640
miners can move around safely and efficiently over 1,000 feet underground.

574
00:36:59,580 --> 00:37:00,940
Here's a game changer.

575
00:37:00,941 --> 00:37:08,340
In July 2021, Chinese engineers showcase what could one day be the world's fastest locomotive.

576
00:37:10,360 --> 00:37:15,700
a bullet train that is designed to reach speeds of almost 375 miles per hour.

577
00:37:17,340 --> 00:37:22,380
It's only a prototype for now, but the technology is called maglev.

578
00:37:23,240 --> 00:37:26,500
And it already powers one of the fastest trains in the world,

579
00:37:27,160 --> 00:37:31,520
a 19-mile-long ride that connects the airport in Shanghai, China,

580
00:37:31,521 --> 00:37:36,860
to the city's metro system at speeds of 267 miles per hour.

581
00:37:36,861 --> 00:37:41,200
But unlike France's fastest train, the TGV,

582
00:37:41,780 --> 00:37:44,340
this one isn't a traditional locomotive.

583
00:37:46,940 --> 00:37:49,380
An engine doesn't power the maglev.

584
00:37:51,060 --> 00:37:52,700
It's powered by the track.

585
00:37:53,760 --> 00:37:57,560
Paradoxically, the maglev train never even comes in contact with it.

586
00:37:58,220 --> 00:38:00,760
It's basically riding on a cushion of air.

587
00:38:01,340 --> 00:38:04,620
It's propelled by invisible magnetic forces.

588
00:38:04,621 --> 00:38:10,180
The locomotive, if you will, is magnetically levitated, propelled, and guided.

589
00:38:10,600 --> 00:38:13,800
On General Atomic's test track in San Diego, California,

590
00:38:15,040 --> 00:38:19,260
engineers built a prototype system designed around the same basic law of physics

591
00:38:19,261 --> 00:38:21,840
as the maglev systems in Asia.

592
00:38:23,100 --> 00:38:29,400
Take some magnets, make a special track, apply power, and it's up, up, and away.

593
00:38:29,401 --> 00:38:36,580
The maglev track, or guideway, is lined with a cluster of electric power cables called

594
00:38:36,581 --> 00:38:40,120
a linear synchronous motor, or LSM.

595
00:38:40,121 --> 00:38:45,040
The train carriage is joined to a set of magnets that wrap around the track.

596
00:38:45,041 --> 00:38:50,600
When the linear synchronous motor is energized, it generates a moving magnetic field, creating

597
00:38:50,601 --> 00:38:54,020
a magnetic wave that pulls the train carriage forward.

598
00:38:54,021 --> 00:38:58,900
To increase speed, more power is added to the LSM.

599
00:38:58,901 --> 00:39:03,500
As the carriage moves forward, the electricity shuts down behind it.

600
00:39:03,501 --> 00:39:06,200
That leaves the issue of levitation.

601
00:39:06,201 --> 00:39:09,160
Let me tell you about how the levitation system works.

602
00:39:09,161 --> 00:39:14,000
We get levitation by virtue of currents that are induced in this track.

603
00:39:14,001 --> 00:39:17,720
The magnets are in this wraparound structure.

604
00:39:17,721 --> 00:39:20,100
They're inside these cans.

605
00:39:20,101 --> 00:39:24,460
And we use a set of magnets for levitation.

606
00:39:24,461 --> 00:39:31,560
the levitation to occur, the magnets are aligned in a formation called the Hullback Array.

607
00:39:31,561 --> 00:39:38,060
The way the levitation system here is going to work is that these arrows indicate the

608
00:39:38,061 --> 00:39:39,827
polarity of the magnets.

609
00:39:39,828 --> 00:39:44,760
That basic configuration is nothing more than each of these magnets

610
00:39:44,761 --> 00:39:46,832
is rotated by 45 degrees.

611
00:39:46,833 --> 00:39:52,220
What it winds up doing is it produces a sine wave that is focused

612
00:39:52,221 --> 00:39:58,400
on the track, it goes in through here, it returns off here, it hugs the backside and

613
00:39:58,401 --> 00:40:04,800
comes out and it tends to focus the magnetic field on the track where you want it.

614
00:40:04,801 --> 00:40:10,560
In layman's terms, what that means is that the wheels are going to lift off the track.

615
00:40:10,561 --> 00:40:14,100
All the magnets need is some forward motion.

616
00:40:14,101 --> 00:40:17,140
On the track, the electric cables provide it.

617
00:40:17,141 --> 00:40:20,060
And here, the spinning wheel induces the power.

618
00:40:20,061 --> 00:40:26,060
And actually what you're seeing right now is that basic principle of levitation.

619
00:40:26,061 --> 00:40:32,120
We're levitated to well over an inch and the levitation force is maintained as long as

620
00:40:32,121 --> 00:40:34,140
there's forward motion.

621
00:40:34,141 --> 00:40:40,300
When we're coming into a station and we're slowing down, then we're going to gradually

622
00:40:40,301 --> 00:40:45,420
get less and less of an air gap until we're almost at the station here.

623
00:40:45,421 --> 00:40:51,300
As we come in, we're just about now landing on the wheels and we come to a stop.

624
00:40:51,301 --> 00:40:56,480
Build up enough speed and it's time for the landing wheels to lift off.

625
00:40:56,481 --> 00:41:02,540
When the speed of the vehicle reaches approximately 10 miles per hour, enough current is induced

626
00:41:02,541 --> 00:41:09,340
in the track that it produces levitation of the entire structure, including the magnets

627
00:41:09,341 --> 00:41:12,920
as well as the wheels here.

628
00:41:12,921 --> 00:41:17,220
By levitating the carriage above the track, you've got a frictionless system that's the

629
00:41:17,221 --> 00:41:22,100
key to generating speeds up to 350 miles per hour.

630
00:41:22,101 --> 00:41:28,360
Like the Maglevs in Asia, the proposed system would require its own dedicated track.

631
00:41:28,361 --> 00:41:32,580
The separate track is also one of Maglevs' advantages.

632
00:41:32,581 --> 00:41:34,580
No rail crossings.

633
00:41:34,581 --> 00:41:38,420
There's also no need for an engineer.

634
00:41:38,421 --> 00:41:43,360
The system is completely automated, run from a control room, where all operators have to

635
00:41:43,361 --> 00:41:48,360
do is program the motor to induce the right amount of power ahead of the carriage.

636
00:41:48,361 --> 00:41:53,080
But the General Atomic's proposed system has one major difference from the high-speed Asian

637
00:41:53,081 --> 00:41:54,080
versions.

638
00:41:54,081 --> 00:41:59,880
We're focusing our technology on lower-speed operation, urban systems, up to about 100

639
00:41:59,881 --> 00:42:01,160
miles per hour.

640
00:42:01,161 --> 00:42:08,120
We feel that there is a real market for alleviating much of the congestion that we find in many

641
00:42:08,121 --> 00:42:10,320
of our cities.

642
00:42:10,321 --> 00:42:13,220
As of today, the United States has not yet built

643
00:42:13,221 --> 00:42:16,320
any commercial maglev trains.

644
00:42:16,321 --> 00:42:19,300
But Japan may soon replace China for the new world's record

645
00:42:19,301 --> 00:42:21,040
holder.

646
00:42:21,041 --> 00:42:25,240
A maglev train running 178 miles between the cities of Tokyo

647
00:42:25,241 --> 00:42:29,040
and Nagoya is planned to open in 2027,

648
00:42:29,041 --> 00:42:34,420
achieving maximum speeds of 314 miles per hour.

649
00:42:34,421 --> 00:42:37,720
Magnets, they're the future of the locomotive.

